kosha tolerance

  -- the permissible variation from a nominal dimension.
  -- no manufacturing process is perfect. tolerance defines acceptable imperfection.
  -- tolerance = upper limit − lower limit.
  -- e.g. 50 ± 0.1 mm: acceptable range 49.9 to 50.1 mm. tolerance = 0.2 mm.
  --
  -- bilateral tolerance: ± — variation allowed in both directions.
  -- unilateral tolerance: +0/-0.1 — variation in one direction only.
  -- limit dimensions: stated as upper and lower bounds directly (50.1 / 49.9).
  --
  -- fits (shaft in hole):
  --   clearance fit:   shaft always smaller than hole. gap guaranteed. easy assembly.
  --                    running fits (H7/f7), sliding fits (H7/g6).
  --   interference fit: shaft larger than hole. must be pressed or shrunk in.
  --                    force fit (H7/p6), shrink fit (H7/s6). permanent assembly.
  --   transition fit:  may be clearance or interference depending on actual sizes.
  --                    locating fits (H7/k6, H7/m6).
  --
  -- ISO tolerance grades: IT01 to IT18. IT1 = finest (precision instruments).
  -- IT6-IT7: general engineering. IT11-IT12: rough machining.
  --
  -- GD&T (Geometric Dimensioning and Tolerancing):
  --   controls not just size but form, orientation, location, runout.
  --   flatness: surface must lie within two parallel planes.
  --   roundness: cross-section must lie within two concentric circles.
  --   position: axis must lie within a cylindrical tolerance zone.
  --   perpendicularity, parallelism, angularity: orientation tolerances.
  --   true position ⊕: location of a hole or feature within a circular zone.

